    "chunk_size_bytes": 1356,
    "chunk_text": "If the Parliament-appointed panel, headed by Supreme Court Justice Preethi Padman Surasena, finds Tennakoon liable, Parliament will be required to vote on his removal. Both the government and the opposition are united in their efforts to sack Tennakoon.\n\nAmid the drama involving Tennakoon, the fate of his deputy, Senior Deputy Inspector-General Nilantha Jayawardena, also hangs in the balance. Jayawardena is facing a disciplinary inquiry over his role in failing to prevent the 2019 Easter Sunday bombings.\n\nAt the time, Jayawardena headed the State Intelligence Service (SIS), which had been alerted to a potential terror strike 17 days before the bombings that targeted three hotels and three churches, killing at least 279 people and injuring over 500.\n\nThe former SIS chief was removed from his position and made the de facto number two in the police force by Wickremesinghe in January 2023. However, on July 18, 2024, the National Police Commission interdicted Jayawardena and launched an investigation.\n\nOfficial sources indicate that it is highly likely Jayawardena will also face termination due to the weight of evidence and several judicial orders calling for his prosecution on charges of incompetence and negligence.\n\nHe had already paid 75 million rupees in damages as ordered by the Supreme Court on January 12, 2023. (COLOMBO/July19/2025)"
