  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2025-07-21-adaderanalk-10fa513d",
    "num": "adaderanalk-10fa513d",
    "date_str": "2025-07-21",
    "description": "Sri Lanka to procure US West Texas Intermediate crude oil?",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.adaderana.lk/news/110683/sri-lanka-to-procure-us-west-texas-intermediate-crude-oil",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "adaderanalk",
    "time_ut": 1753082700.0,
    "chunk_id": "2025-07-21-adaderanalk-10fa513d-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "a1468c299c8c9c3b0c6b53f3b0e733a4",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1930,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka to procure US West Texas Intermediate crude oil?\n\nThe government is reportedly looking at the possibility of procuring US West Texas Intermediate (WTI) crude oil in a bid to reduce the trade deficit between Sri Lanka and the United States.\n\nFollowing the decision by US President Donald Trump to impose reciprocal tariffs based on trade deficit between the US and other trading partners, several countries including Sri Lanka have been engaging with the Office of the United States Trade Representative to strike deals to reduce the tariff rates.\n\nAlthough US President Trump announced a tariff rate of 44% in April for Sri Lankan exports, it has now been reduced to 30%, effective August 1.\n\nLocal officials are continuing to hold discussions in a bid to reduce the tariff rate further, according to government sources.\n\nAccordingly, Managing Director of the Ceylon Petroleum Corporation Dr. Mayura Neththikumarage noted that discussions are currently being held to include US West Texas Intermediate crude oil in the tender process when procuring oil.\n\nWhen inquired by Ada Derana, Dr. Mayura Neththikumarage stated that at present, Sri Lanka procures crude oil stocks that originate from the Gulf region, however a decision has been made to include U.S.-registered companies involved in West Texas Intermediate crude oil sales in future tender process.\n\nHe however noted that a decision will be made on which type of crude oil to import to Sri Lanka based on price considerations.\n\nDr. Mayura Neththikumarage added that Sri Lanka will also benefit, if the US decides to reduce its tariff rates which will have a bearing on both imports as well as exports.\n\nThe Chairman of the Export Development Board (EDB), Mangala Wijesinghe, stated that discussions will be held with the United States before August 1, in an effort to obtain further relief from the 30% reciprocal tariffs imposed on goods exported from Sri Lanka."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2025-07-21-adaderanalk-10fa513d",
