    "chunk_size_bytes": 1853,
    "chunk_text": "Sadly, one can argue\u2014with due respect to the victims and the campaigners for justice\u2014that the ongoing probe into the Easter Sunday tragedy has come to resemble a \u2018mega teledrama\u2019 with new episodes added to it endlessly. The Channel 04 programme on the Easter Sunday attacks is a case in point; it is full of sensational, unsubstantiated claims.\n\nThe government has, in its wisdom, brought former SSP Shani Abeysekera, a prominent member of the ruling NPP\u2019s Retired Police Collective (RPC), out of retirement and appointed him as the Director of the CID. It has also elevated retired SDIG Ravi Seneviratne, the Head of the RPC of the NPP, to the position of Secretary to the Ministry of Public Security. Both of them were in charge of the CID at the time of the Easter Sunday attacks. The police are not short of capable men and women of integrity who can probe the Easter Sunday attacks impartially and efficiently. Why did the government pull Seneviratne and Abeysekera out of retirement and appoint them to high posts?\n\nMinister and Leader of the House Bimal Rathnayake told Parliament during a recent debate on the Easter Sunday attacks that Archbishop of Colombo Malcolm Cardinal Ranjith had, on behalf of the Catholic community, requested that Seneviratne and Abeysekera be brought in to handle the probe into the Easter Sunday carnage. But Spokesman for the Archdiocese of Colombo Fr. Cyril Gamini Fernando yesterday told the media that the Archbishop of Colombo had made no such request. He said Cardinal Ranjith had only requested that the probe be entrusted to the investigators sidelined by the Gotabaya Rajapaksa government. Fr. Fernando insisted that the Archbishop of Colombo had not mentioned any names. Did the government use the prelate\u2019s request and make the aforementioned appointments to advance a hidden political agenda?"
