    "md5": "da6c992766bff531c740c78156325605",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1804,
    "chunk_text": "Apart from war, there is violence in this world. War in fact is an extension of violent mindset of the humans irrespective of country, region, culture, religion and the rich-poor differences. This is what we must address. Conflicts and violence are present in families, society, countries and internationally. Women are most vulnerable in domestic violence. From the Buddha, Jesus Christ and to Prophet Muhammad there are religious leaders who have attempted to address this issue. However, their followers have largely deviated even in creating conflicts among themselves.\n\nDuring the modern period (since 1500) there were several ideologies particularly in the West. Liberalism and Marxism were the two main streams. Marxism advocated class struggle, and war or violence could be justified under certain circumstances. Liberalism was ambiguous and different views were expressed by different theoreticians. In the case of Bertrand Russell, although his general views were against war and violence, he had to justify the war against fascism. Even in the case of theory (Why Men Fight), he expressed that some human impulses and desires lead the humans to become violent. In the Asian context, Mahatma Ghandi and his philosophy would be one of the best in advocating peace and non-violence.\n\nThe United Nations can be considered the foremost organization that was formed after the Second World War to establish and maintain peace and security in the world. This is very clear from the Preamble and the Articles of the United Nations Charter. However, it is a governmental organization. And as a result, there are limitations to the organization and its affiliated entities. Nevertheless, no one should disregard this organization, and maximum opportunities should be utilized to build peace in the world."
