    "chunk_size_bytes": 1891,
    "chunk_text": "Selective amnesia\n\nSri Lankan politicians are notorious for feigned amnesia. Their memory is as sharp as a hawk\u2019s vision at noon when they delve into past incidents that they can use to their advantage. The UNP has forgotten its barbaric counterterror operations, torture chambers, abductions, and ubiquitous tyre-pyres in the late 1980s. But its current members and their erstwhile chums in the SJB, etc., often hark back to the JVP\u2019s second uprising characterised by mindless violence. The JVP vividly recalls atrocities and injustices it suffered during the past six decades or so, but cannot remember a single incident where it unleashed barbaric violence against civilians in relentless pursuit of political conformity.\n\nThe other political parties are no better. The SLPP has a remarkable ability to recall the incidents of JVP terror, but does not remember the emblematic crimes committed while its leaders were in power. The TNA revisits the war-time atrocities the armed forces are blamed for, while keeping silent on the countless massacres the LTTE committed. The country has just witnessed the JVP\u2019s latest bout of selective memory loss.\n\nOn Wednesday (23) Minister of Public Security Ananda Wijepala told Parliament, in answer to a question raised by SJB MP Rohini Kaviratne, that no records were available about the 1989 attack on the Dalada Maligawa during the JVP\u2019s reign of terror, except the arrest of six female suspects. On several occasions, the JVP has denied any involvement in the terror attack on the Dalada Maligawa. But Neranjan Wijeratne, who was the Diyawadena Nilame at the time of the attack, has vehemently rejected the JVP\u2019s denials. When the issue resurfaced in the run-up to the 2001 general election, Wijeratne was quoted by The Island (on 25 November 2001) as saying that the JVP was responsible for that terror strike which claimed five lives."
