    "md5": "ae83c6c35f0cc53abe08a049e99962ec",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1902,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka\u2019s central bank has operated broadly deflationary policy since the third quarter of 2022 providing sound money in classical economic terms, allowing growth to recover and improving public confidence in government policy.\n\nSri Lanka is also planning to boost capex in the near future partly with foreign financed loans.\n\nHowever there has been warnings that the central bank is skirting on thin ice with the latest rate cut and further rate cuts may make it difficult to repay foreign debt as happened after 2015.\n\nRELATED :  Sri Lanka central bank skating on thin default ice with latest rate cut\n\nAnalysts have warned that printing money in the last quarter of 2024 to target a mid corridor rate (rates went up due to a pick up in private credit) and the last rate cut as bank deposit rates went up, are red flags.\n\nWhen private credit picks up, banks have tended to pay less attention to government debt in the past.\n\nSri Lanka usually triggers currency crisis not due to budget deficits per se, but due to the unwillingness by the central bank to allow Treasuries yields to move up when private credit recovers, leading to monetizing debt, not just from current budgets, but maturing securities from past deficits, analysts have shown.\n\nRELATED : Sri Lanka\u2019s deficit lie: the inflationist narrative that leads to currency crises and default\n\nThis week a Treasury bill auction failed to be fully sold but there were more subscriptions on tap. The rejection of securities is a also type of \u2018signalling\u2019 mechanism which is bound to fail when private credit really picks up fail analysts have warned.\n\nAnalysts have also pointed out that it is not really possible to manipulate gilt yields for a \u2018transmission mechanism\u2019 with a \u2018domestic buffer\u2019 when private credit grows strongly.\n\nRELATED : Sri Lanka Treasury should buy its own dollars to settle debt and avoid second default"
  },
