    "description": "IMF urges strong revenue measures in 2026 Budget to support Sri Lanka\u2019s macroeconomic stability",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.adaderana.lk/news/110832/imf-urges-strong-revenue-measures-in-2026-budget-to-support-sri-lankas-macroeconomic-stability",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "adaderanalk",
    "time_ut": 1753425540.0,
    "chunk_id": "2025-07-25-adaderanalk-2a492eb2-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "c0da9c9454decbfc85fbcd32d5d972ba",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1943,
    "chunk_text": "IMF urges strong revenue measures in 2026 Budget to support Sri Lanka\u2019s macroeconomic stability\n\nAn International Monetary Fund (IMF) mission team, led by Evan Papageorgiou, IMF Mission Chief for Sri Lanka, concluded its visit to Colombo from July 21 to 25, 2025.\n\nThe mission was undertaken to assess recent macroeconomic developments and evaluate progress in implementing the economic and financial policies under Sri Lanka\u2019s reform agenda, supported by the IMF\u2019s Extended Fund Facility (EFF) arrangement.\n\nIn an end of mission statement, the IMF team noted that the economic reforms implemented by the Sri Lankan authorities are bearing fruit, with economic growth outperforming expectations, inflation moving toward target, external reserves accumulating, and fiscal revenues improving.\n\nThe statement emphasized that sustaining the reform momentum is critical to safeguarding macroeconomic stability, entrenching the recovery, and building Sri Lanka\u2019s resilience to shocks, especially in the context of ongoing trade policy uncertainty and heightened geopolitical tensions.\n\nProgress in meeting key commitments under the IMF-supported program will be formally assessed in the context of the upcoming Fifth Review of the Extended Fund Facility (EFF). The timing of the review will be discussed with the government, it said.\n\nAdditionally, as Sri Lanka prepares its 2026 budget, the IMF has strongly emphasized the need for decisive revenue-enhancing measures to maintain macroeconomic stability and ensure continued progress under the reform program.\n\nThe Fund highlighted that achieving the medium-term primary balance target of 2.3 percent of GDP is essential for restoring debt sustainability.\n\n\u201cStrengthening the tax exemption frameworks, boosting tax compliance, broadening the tax base, and enhancing public financial management\u2014including to avoid the reemergence of expenditure arrears\u2014are important,\u201d the IMF noted."
  },
