    "md5": "2645f32920731ac829f8158309365a07",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1671,
    "chunk_text": "Law, religion and politics\n\nA Magistrate is reported to have told a Buddhist monk, produced before him in a case against the construction of an unauthorised building in a railway reservation in the Gampaha District, a home truth. Ordering that the illegal structure be demolished within one month, the judge stressed that religion and the law must not be confused with one another, according to media reports.\n\nThe conflation of religious faith with immunity from legal accountability amounts to a distortion of both religion and governance, and leads to a severe erosion of public faith in the rule of law. Above all, the construction of places of worship and the erection of various religious statues in places such as key traffic nodes, hindering vehicle movement, and preventing road development, is tantamount to a misuse of spiritual authority. Religious leaders ought to lead by example.\n\nNow that the Railway Department has fought a successful legal battle against the aforementioned monk, it ought to ensure that all those who have encroached on railway reservations across the country are similarly dealt with. There are hundreds, if not thousands, of houses and kiosks, located in close proximity to railroads, especially in the suburbs of Colombo. It is no secret that these encroachers leverage their block votes, numbering thousands, to pressure politicians to intervene to prevent the Railway Department from evicting them. The same holds true for illegal settlements on canal banks in Colombo. Vote-bank politics, as it were, takes precedence over the rule of law in this country. So does the immense lobbying power of the financiers of political parties."
  },
