    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "b3d21c632a728401f94641d7008af8e0",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1419,
    "chunk_text": "BYD in Singapore sells an Atto 3 model with a 100kW rated motor to conform to what is called a Category A certificate of entitlement in Singapore, according to the company website.\n\nA lower rated motor means its performance will be lower, and for example may not be able to accelerate as quickly as the one with the higher output motor.\n\nConventional automakers also sell cars the same model car with a range of engine capacities, but the size of the engine can be readily ascertained.\n\nThe practice of limiting output of motors in electric cars is new to Sri Lanka and there is uncertainty on what the decision will be.\n\nThe excise for a 100 KW motor vehicle is 2.4 million rupees, but this rises to about 5.4 million rupees for a 150 KW vehicle.\n\nIf it is determined that the tax applicable for a 150kW car is charged on the 100kW vehicle, a buyer may have to pay about 4 million rupees extra, an official source said.\n\nAlready over 1,000 vehicles have been released to end users.\n\nAt a meeting of the Parliament\u2019s Committee on Public Finance earlier this year, some participants raised the matter that the same model car was being charged different taxes based on the motor output declared in the documents submitted by the manufacturer.\n\nThe higher taxed car appeared to have been imported as a used car from a market where 150kW BYDs are sold, which has led to the controversy. (Colombo/July 29/2025 \u2013 Updated)"
  },
