    "chunk_text": "Big debate on education reforms: Are we helping or hurting our children?\n\nA major debate is unfolding on the government\u2019s proposed changes to Sri Lanka\u2019s education system. Led by Prime Minister and Education Minister Dr. Harini Amarasuriya, the reforms aim to change what subjects that students must study and what they can choose. While the plan promises more flexibility with new elective subjects, many parents, teachers, and experts are concerned. The big question now is\u2014will these reforms truly benefit our children, or will they widen the gap between urban and rural schools?\n\nWhile education reform is not a new phenomenon, its urgency has been intensified by global challenges such as the COVID-19 pandemic, digital transformation, widening social inequalities, and the need for future-ready skills. In this context, education is no longer limited to literacy or schooling\u2014it must enable individuals to navigate complexity, think critically, collaborate across cultures, and adapt to rapid change. Consequently, many countries have launched reforms that focus on curriculum innovation, digital integration, inclusive education, and learner-centred pedagogy.\n\nIn recent years, the Government of Sri Lanka has proposed successive waves of reform, most notably under the leadership of Dr. Susil Premajayantha (2022) and Dr. Harini Amarasuriya (2025). These reform agendas reflect a growing recognition of the need to modernise general, higher, and vocational education. While the 2022 proposals emphasised outcomes-based planning, technological adaptation, and stakeholder engagement, the 2025 roadmap focuses on curriculum reform, human resource development, infrastructure, and systematic assessment. (See Table 1)\n\nThe 2022 reforms are stronger in calling for public engagement and participatory planning. By contrast, the 2025 reforms lean toward top-down planning, which may limit buy-in from communities, teachers, and students."
