    "md5": "2c29294f2bfbe76321e53aa3c38513f2",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1742,
    "chunk_text": "The 2022 reforms are stronger in calling for public engagement and participatory planning. By contrast, the 2025 reforms lean toward top-down planning, which may limit buy-in from communities, teachers, and students.\n\nReforming an education system is not just about fixing what\u2019s broken\u2014it\u2019s about reimagining the purpose of education in shaping a just, sustainable, and dynamic society. Sri Lanka has the legacy of free education and the intellectual capital to lead South Asia in educational transformation.\n\nBoth reform initiatives offer valuable foundations, but their success will depend on:\n\nThese reforms must not merely be administrative exercises or infrastructure drives. They must produce engaged learners, reflective teachers, inclusive schools, and a generation ready to lead Sri Lanka into the 21st century.\n\nGlobally, education reform has often taken shape under the influence of international organisations like the OECD, UNESCO, and World Bank, which have promoted frameworks such as Education for All (EFA) and the Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs). The OECD\u2019s PISA assessments have particularly influenced curriculum reforms by promoting evidence-based policymaking and comparative performance metrics.\n\nIn many countries, reforms focus on outcome-based education (OBE), competency-based curricula, and school autonomy. For instance, Finland has been recognised for its holistic and child-centred approach, emphasising equity, teacher autonomy, and trust-based accountability. Conversely, the United States has oscillated between centralization and decentralization in reform, with programmes like No Child Left Behind (2001) and Every Student Succeeds Act (2015) promoting standards, testing, and school choice."
