    "chunk_size_bytes": 1577,
    "chunk_text": "In many countries, reforms focus on outcome-based education (OBE), competency-based curricula, and school autonomy. For instance, Finland has been recognised for its holistic and child-centred approach, emphasising equity, teacher autonomy, and trust-based accountability. Conversely, the United States has oscillated between centralization and decentralization in reform, with programmes like No Child Left Behind (2001) and Every Student Succeeds Act (2015) promoting standards, testing, and school choice.\n\nHowever, in many contexts, especially in developing countries, implementation remains exam-driven, reinforcing rote learning and limiting creative engagement. This has led to concerns about the \u201cimplementation gap\u201d, where well-intentioned reforms fail due to capacity limitations, resistance from stakeholders, or inadequate funding.\n\nHere\u2019s an enhanced table with color-coded categories and a focus on South Asia alongside global comparators. This version helps lay readers quickly understand where each country stands on religion in school curricula: (See Table 2)\n\n\ud83d\udfe5 Secular \u2013 No religion taught in state schools (e.g., France, USA, India)\n\n\u2013 Single dominant religion embedded in national curriculum (e.g., Saudi Arabia, Pakistan)\n\n\ud83d\udfe6 Multi-faith \u2013 Multiple religions offered based on student affiliation (e.g., Sri Lanka, Bangladesh)\n\n\ud83d\udfe8 Hybrid \u2013 Combination of religion and ethics/moral reasoning with student/parent choice (e.g., Germany, Norway)\n\n\ud83d\udd36 Optional \u2013 Religious education not mandatory, based on parental choice or school type"
