    "language": "en",
    "md5": "c5b24e7435d2e8d1fd3579d777937ce4",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1762,
    "chunk_text": "Digital Services Taxes (DSTs) emerged as a way for countries to assert their right to tax the digital economy, especially in response to perceived tax avoidance by large tech firms like Google, Amazon, and Meta. These companies often shifted profits to low-tax jurisdictions, exploiting outdated tax rules based on physical presence. In the absence of a modern global tax framework, countries introduced DSTs to capture revenue from digital activities within their borders. However, these unilateral taxes sparked opposition from the US, which viewed them as discriminatory and retaliated with trade measures.\n\nDST targets the gross revenues of large multinational digital firms derived from specific services within a jurisdiction such as online advertising, social networking, or monetisation of user data etc. These taxes are designed to address concerns about profit shifting and the under-taxation of digital giants that operate extensively in a country without a physical presence. Unlike VAT, DSTs are not levied on consumers but directly on corporate revenues, making them more controversial in international trade discussions.\n\nIt is important to emphasise that Sri Lanka\u2019s 18% levy is a digital VAT, not a DST. This distinction is critical, as US trade investigations and retaliatory actions have been directed exclusively at DST regimes, which are perceived as discriminatory toward US-based tech companies. In contrast, countries that apply digital VATs in accordance with international norms have not faced similar scrutiny or sanctions from the United States. It is significant to note that currently over 100 countries are levying VAT on cross border Digital Services.\n\nSection 301 of the Trade Act of 1974: A strategic US trade enforcement tool"
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2025-07-30-dailyftlk-f7229cb2",
    "num": "dailyftlk-f7229cb2",
    "date_str": "2025-07-30",
    "description": "Digital VAT, DST, retaliatory taxes and US laws",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/columns/Digital-VAT-DST-retaliatory-taxes-and-US-laws/4-779656",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
