    "chunk_text": "The Reserve comprises a diverse mosaic of mangrove forests, saltmarshes, mudflats, seagrass beds, and coral reefs, forming one of the most biologically and economically significant natural landscapes in the country. At its core lies Sri Lanka\u2019s largest continuous mangrove forest directly facing the sea, a rare coastal ecosystem that acts as a vital buffer against storm surges, coastal erosion, and sea-level rise. These mangroves are also crucial carbon sinks, contributing significantly to climate change mitigation.\n\nWhen the case was taken up on 29 July 2025 before Justices S. Thurairaja, PC, Yasantha Kodagoda, PC, and K. Priyantha Fernando, Attorney-at-Law Nilshantha Sirimanne, together with Deshara Goonetilleke, appeared on behalf of the Petitioner (EFL). Additional Solicitor General Viveka Siriwardena, PC, appearing for the Respondents, informed Court that the Cabinet of Ministers had decided on 7 July 2025 to revoke the said Gazette (No.2383/05).\n\nShe further informed Court that a new Gazette rescinding the earlier one would be issued in due course and this case is to be mentioned again on 10 October 2025 in order to ascertain compliance with the said undertaking given by the Attorney General."
