    "md5": "28a81eaef24780477977345cb6398030",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1354,
    "chunk_text": "This disparity cannot be attributed to taxes or transport costs alone; it exposes the deep inefficiencies and profiteering embedded within the supply chain. With input costs for fertiliser, fuel, and labour steadily rising, many smallholders now struggle to break even. Some have already reduced their output or left the sector entirely posing a serious threat to the long-term sustainability of the industry.\n\nBy comparison, other key Sri Lankan commodities operate with greater fairness and efficiency. The tea industry, for instance, benefits from auctions aligned with international grading standards. Rubber pricing is indexed to transparent global benchmarks in Bangkok and Singapore. These models promote quality, ensure transparency, and deliver better value to producers. The coconut auction system, however, remains opaque and outdated failing both farmers and the broader economy.\n\nWhile some argue including state that low auction prices help protect domestic consumers, this justification falters in the face of persistently high retail prices and declining farmgate earnings. In reality, it is the intermediaries\u2019 brokers and large buyers who benefit most from the current structure. Without meaningful reform, this imbalance will continue to erode rural incomes and undermine Sri Lanka\u2019s competitiveness in the global coconut industry."
  },
