    "md5": "2ff6299236fbf51379319dcfaa563330",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1953,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka to facilitate imports from the US, anti-graft helps reduce Trump tariffs\n\nECONOMYNEXT \u2013 Sri Lanka will facilitate more imports from the United States to reduce a trade deficit and also make it easier for American firms to do business in the country, Treasury Secretary Harshana Suriyapperuma said.\n\nSri Lanka negotiated a reduction of the Trump tariff from 44 to 20 percent in two levels.\n\nSri Lanka has achieved the first objective of maintaining competition position with other exporting nations, Suriyapperuma who led the negotiations said.\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s strategy to reduce corruption and help clear a path for companies to do business in the island had gone a long way to reduce the tariff, he said.\n\nDue to non-disclosure rules Sri Lanka cannot immediately release all areas that are under discussion, but the country has been as open as possible, he said.\n\n\u201cSome of them are the ones you very correctly identified,\u201d Suriyapperuma said, responding to a question whether the US Trade Representative\u2019s long standing positions on intellectual property protection and agricultural market access had been discussed and whether imports of oil would go ahead using quotas.\n\n\u201cSri Lanka is a net importer of energy into the country,\u201d he said. \u201cSo wherever possible, within competitive parameters to see how best we can accommodate their requirements.\u201d\n\nSri Lanka had also discussed hi-tec goods imports and knowledge transfer and there will be government to government negotiations he said.\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s Ceylon Petroleum Corporation had said it was looking into importing oil from the US.\n\nInstead of only reducing rates, Sri Lanka had offered to do trade facilitation which had been a \u2018pain point\u2019 for some exporters of some countries, he said.\n\nSri Lanka was separately drawing up a national tariff policy.\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s efforts to combat corruption had scored points in helping bring down the tariff, Suriyapperuma said."
