    "md5": "181fc57a9bfb4f910b3ef561ebbc5014",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1658,
    "chunk_text": "Trump\u2019s Nobel dream\n\nUS President Donald Trump\u2019s dreams are in the news again. It was a dream come true for him to secure a non-consecutive second term. Not many expected him to be re-elected after the 2021 mob attacks carried out by his supporters on the Capitol. Then came the felony conviction which, his critics thought, would ruin his chances of re-election, but he realised his dream of savouring power again. There seems to be no end to his dreaming. Now, he is eyeing the Nobel Peace Prize.\n\nLast month, he himself said that he deserved it for having ended conflicts in different parts of the world, especially between India and Pakistan. India has denied his claim. White House Press Secretary Karoline Leavitt said, on Thursday, President Trump had ended six conflicts between Thailand and Cambodia, Israel and Iran, Rwanda and the Democratic Republic of Congo, India and Pakistan, Serbia and Kosovo, and Egypt and Ethiopia. He brokered one peace deal or ceasefire per month and it was \u2018well past time that he should be awarded the Nobel Peace Prize\u2019, she said.\n\nTrump has demonstrated his aversion to war and intervened to stop some conflicts, but how prudent is it to award the Nobel Peace Prize to a US President before his retirement? What guarantee is there that Trump will not start wars? He had Iran\u2019s nuclear facilities bombed, didn\u2019t he?\n\nPresident Barack Obama was awarded the Nobel Peace Prize in 2009, but the US, under his watch, backed the invasion of Libya, making that country descend into a failed state, fractured by rival militias, extremist groups, arms traffickers, human smugglers and various other criminal gangs."
  },
